                    <h2 class="head">TOURISM.</h2>
                    <p>If you’re looking for a destination that doesn’t just show you sights but makes you feel alive, Nigeria is calling your name. This isn't a "sit back and watch" kind of holiday—it’s an all-encompassing, high-energy, soul-stirring adventure.
Here is why Nigeria should be your next great escape:</p>
                     <h5>LANDSCAPES</h5>
                     <ul>
                        <li>Obudu Mountain Resort: Ride a cable car through the clouds into a temperate mountain paradise that feels more like the Swiss Alps than West Africa.</li>
                        <li>Yankari Game Reserve: After a morning safari spotting elephants, you can dive into the Wikki Warm Springs—crystal-clear, turquoise water that stays a perfect 31°C year-round.</li>
                        <li>The Waterfalls: Hike the seven levels of Erin Ijesha. Each level reveals a new, more stunning view than the last.</li>
                     </ul>  
                     
                        <h5>NIGERIAN CUISINE</h5>
                        <h7>The Southwest (Yoruba)</h7>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Amala & Ewedu: A dark yam-flour swallow served with a green jute-leaf soup and a spicy tomato-based stew (Gbegiri).</li>
                            <li>Efo Riro: A savory "rich" vegetable soup prepared with spinach, palm oil, and various proteins like cow skin (Ponmo).</li>
                            <li>Ewa Agoyin: Overcooked beans served with a signature dark, caramelized pepper sauce.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <h7>The Southeast & South-South (Igbo, Efik, Ibibio) </h7>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Afang & Edikang Ikong: These are nutrient-dense vegetable powerhouses. Afang is made from wild spinach, while Edikang Ikong uses fluted pumpkin leaves.</li>
                            <li>Banga Soup: A creamy palm nut-based soup originating from the Niger Delta, often served with a yellow starch swallow.</li>
                            <li>Nkwobi: A spicy cow-foot delicacy served as an appetizer, usually enjoyed in local pubs.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <h7>The North (Hausa-Fulani)</h7>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Tuwo Shinkafa: A soft, mashed rice swallow that is a staple in northern homes.</li>
                            <li>Miyan Kuka: A unique green soup made from powdered baobab leaves, known for its tangy flavor.</li>
                            <li>Kilishi: The Nigerian version of beef jerky—thin slices of spiced, dried meat that are briefly grilled.</li>
                        </ul>

