            <section id="about">
                <h2 class="head">ABOUT [ THE NIGERIAN CULTURE AND HISTORY].</h2>
                <img src="Image/image1.jpg" alt="tech guy" style="border-radius: 15px;">
                <p>Nigeria, located in West Africa, is the most populous country on the continent with over 236 million people and more than 250 ethnic groups. Often called the "Giant of Africa," it is a diverse nation featuring a mix of rapid urbanization in cities like Lagos and Abuja, alongside traditional rural settlements.</p> 

<h5>Key Ethnic Groups and Peoples</h5>

<p>Hausa-Fulani (North): Predominantly Muslim, they are the largest group, known for their historical emirates, trading, and agriculture.
Yoruba (Southwest): Known for their ancient kingdoms (e.g., Oyo, Ife) and high levels of urbanization, with a mix of Christian, Muslim, and traditional beliefs.
Igbo (Southeast): Historically organized in decentralized, autonomous village units, they are predominantly Christian and known for entrepreneurship.
Other Significant Groups: The Kanuri (northeast), Tiv (North Central), Ibibio, Efik, and Ijaw (south-south) are major ethnic groups contributing to the nation's 500+ languages.</p> 


<h5>Culture and Lifestyle</h5>
    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>Languages: While English is the official language, Hausa, Yoruba, and Igbo are widely spoken, along with Nigerian Pidgin, which acts as a lingua franca in many southern and central cities.</li>
            <li>Religion: The population is roughly divided between Muslims (mainly in the north) and Christians (mainly in the south), with traditional religions also practiced.</li>
            <li>Arts & Entertainment: Nigeria is a global cultural hub, home to Nollywood (the film industry) and Afrobeat music.</li>
            <li>Cuisine: Diverse culinary traditions, including staples like Jollof rice, pounded yam, egusi soup, and suya.</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
